› Write a 300 word essay on mutexes. Do not run any commands or edit any files.


• A mutex, short for mutual exclusion, is a synchronization tool that protects shared data when multiple threads run at
  the same time. Without one, two threads might update a bank balance simultaneously, each reading the old value and
  overwriting the other's work. Such errors are called race conditions because the result depends on unpredictable
  timing.

  A thread locks a mutex before entering a critical section, the small part of a program that accesses shared state.
  While it holds the lock, other threads seeking the same mutex must wait. After completing its work, the thread unlocks
  the mutex, allowing another thread to proceed. This simple rule makes a sequence of operations behave as one protected
  unit.

  Mutexes are useful, but careful design matters. If a thread forgets to unlock, others may wait forever. If two threads
  acquire several mutexes in different orders, each can hold one while waiting for the other, creating deadlock. Even
  correct locking can hurt performance when threads spend too much time waiting instead of working. Programmers
  therefore keep critical sections brief, use a consistent lock order, and release locks reliably, often through
  language features that handle cleanup automatically.

  Choosing what to protect is equally important. A mutex should guard a clear invariant: a condition that must remain
  true whenever no thread is changing the data. For example, a queue's item count should match the number of items it
  contains. Protecting both values with the same mutex keeps that relationship intact.

  Mutexes do not solve every concurrency problem. Some tasks benefit from message passing, immutable data, or atomic
  operations. Still, mutexes remain a foundation because they express a straightforward promise: only one thread may
  change protected state at a time. Used thoughtfully, they turn fragile timing dependent behavior into predictable,
  maintainable code, making shared memory safer for everyone.

